{
  "case_id": "2019-18-8369",
  "document_hash": "e48af37ab840f758e7dbb32fbc35767ad8908dbdeff6a06191dda43fda4f6243",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Brian T. Burgess",
      "case_id": "2019-18-8369",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e48af37ab840f758e7dbb32fbc35767ad8908dbdeff6a06191dda43fda4f6243",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e48af37ab840-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Without-prejudice dismissals for\nfailure to state a claim are not strikes under\nSection 1915(g) for three reasons.\nFirst, the statute uses a familiar\nlegal phrase with a well-established meaning in\nthe context relevant here. When courts review\njudicial orders to determine their impact on a\nfuture action, evaluating preclusion, for\nexample, they conclusively presume that the\nphrase \"dismissed for failure to state a claim\"\nmeans dismissed with prejudice.\nIn 1915(g), Congress used the same\nphrase in the same basic context. There's every\nreason to think Congress expected courts to\napply the phrase's settled meaning rather than"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2019-18-8369",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e48af37ab840f758e7dbb32fbc35767ad8908dbdeff6a06191dda43fda4f6243",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e48af37ab840-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: You -- you are\nassuming that the preclusion question and the\nIFP status go hand in hand, but it could well be\nthat a dismissal without prejudice will not have\npreclusive effect, but, at the same time, it\ncould mean that you have to pay the filing fee.\nYou -- you seem to be assuming that\nthese two go hand in hand, but that's not\nnecessarily so."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2019-18-8369",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e48af37ab840f758e7dbb32fbc35767ad8908dbdeff6a06191dda43fda4f6243",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e48af37ab840-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: I'm just wondering about the incentives that the rule that you're advocating will provide for district courts. In deciding whether to dismiss with prejudice or without prejudice, if they have to take into account -- if I dismiss without prejudice, this is -- is going to enable this frequent litigator to continue to file, will -- do you have any concern that that's going to give them an incentive to label these dismissals with"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2019-18-8369",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e48af37ab840f758e7dbb32fbc35767ad8908dbdeff6a06191dda43fda4f6243",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e48af37ab840-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Well, you think the second one should have been dismissed as frivolous?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2019-18-8369",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e48af37ab840f758e7dbb32fbc35767ad8908dbdeff6a06191dda43fda4f6243",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e48af37ab840-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: That it would have been -- from a litigation fairness point of view, it would have been appropriate for the district court to say you brought a Heck claim once, we dismissed it, and now you did the same thing again, nothing has changed, so it's frivolous, out you go? That's -- that would have been an appropriate solution to this case?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Burgess",
      "case_id": "2019-18-8369",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e48af37ab840f758e7dbb32fbc35767ad8908dbdeff6a06191dda43fda4f6243",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e48af37ab840-0001-0009",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. BURGESS: I think the court would have well been within its discretion to potentially dismiss it as frivolous or malicious."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2019-18-8369",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e48af37ab840f758e7dbb32fbc35767ad8908dbdeff6a06191dda43fda4f6243",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e48af37ab840-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Why -- why do you assume that a procedural defect doesn't tax the resources of the -- of the court?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2019-18-8369",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e48af37ab840f758e7dbb32fbc35767ad8908dbdeff6a06191dda43fda4f6243",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e48af37ab840-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Well, you can -- it -- it may seem odd, but you can -- you have frivolous cases that are dismissed as frivolous without prejudice, right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2019-18-8369",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e48af37ab840f758e7dbb32fbc35767ad8908dbdeff6a06191dda43fda4f6243",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e48af37ab840-0001-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 34,
        "start": 34
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAVANAUGH: Does that -- does that make sense to treat those two functionally-identical things from the prisoner's perspective differently?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2019-18-8369",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e48af37ab840f758e7dbb32fbc35767ad8908dbdeff6a06191dda43fda4f6243",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e48af37ab840-0001-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: So, in those cases where there's a failure to state a claim but the court believes that there's the potential of a legitimate claim, it certainly has the right to dismiss the complaint but with leave to refile?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Olson",
      "case_id": "2019-18-8369",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "document_hash": "e48af37ab840f758e7dbb32fbc35767ad8908dbdeff6a06191dda43fda4f6243",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e48af37ab840-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. OLSON: It's a better rule because of its clarity and administrability. It allows for no uncertainty for -- to determine what happened. And it honors the scope of the other dismissal, so frivolous and malicious can be with and without prejudice. And there are -- there are many examples where there can be some cases where it seems unfair that that was -- was applied, but it is an easily administrable rule."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2019-18-8369",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "document_hash": "e48af37ab840f758e7dbb32fbc35767ad8908dbdeff6a06191dda43fda4f6243",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e48af37ab840-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAVANAUGH: -- can I ask one related question? It's alluded to in Footnote 7 of the reply brief, which is, do you think the PLRA allows sua sponte dismissals without leave to amend?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2019-18-8369",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "document_hash": "e48af37ab840f758e7dbb32fbc35767ad8908dbdeff6a06191dda43fda4f6243",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e48af37ab840-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the consequence -- and I think this goes to a question Justice Kavanaugh asked -- is why would it be a -- a better rule, I think you asked of my colleague from Colorado? Why would it be a better rule is because, if we say that ones without prejudice are not going to be strikes, that means, in effect, Heck dismissals will not count as strikes and inmates may file an unlimited number, in terms of not paying a filing fee, an unlimited number of IFP actions without consequence."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2019-18-8369",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "document_hash": "e48af37ab840f758e7dbb32fbc35767ad8908dbdeff6a06191dda43fda4f6243",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e48af37ab840-0002-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Respectfully, the history since the PLRA was enacted shows -- shows otherwise. At its zenith, 25 percent of the civil docket of -- of the federal courts were prisoner filings, and it is now down to about 10 percent. It's still a very large number, approximately 29,000 a year ago, but it's from 20 -- 25 percent to 10 percent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2019-18-8369",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "document_hash": "e48af37ab840f758e7dbb32fbc35767ad8908dbdeff6a06191dda43fda4f6243",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e48af37ab840-0002-0006",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 61,
        "start": 61
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If the Court has no further questions, we urge you to reverse."
    },
    {
